%I A386683 #25 Nov 04 2026 22:42:59
%S A386683 1,1,1,2,1,2,1,1,3,1,1
%N A386683 Number of unique n-element sum-distinct sets with minimal largest element.
%C A386683 A276661 is the least k such that there is a set S in {1, 2, ..., k} with n elements and the property that each of its subsets has a distinct sum. a(n) is the number of distinct such optimal sets for each n.
%C A386683 Terms up to a(9) computed by Borwein and Mossinghoff.
%C A386683 Conjecture: there will be at least 2 solutions when n = m*(m+1)/2 + 2 (3,5,8,12,17,...) - exactly when A005230(n) = 2*A005230(n-1). In general, if (d(1),...,d(n)) is the difference vector of a set S (elements in decreasing order) that has distinct subset sums, and d(n) = 2*d(n-1), then (d(1),...,d(n-2) + d(n-1),d(n-1),d(n-1)) will also produce a valid solution (confirmed for ~10^7 solutions for n = 4 to 8, and for every entry in A005230).
%C A386683 a(10) = 1 was found by Paul W. Dyson, whose exhaustive search showed that the set given in the example is the only 10-element set with distinct subset sums and largest element A276661(10) = 309. An independent exhaustive search confirms this (see links). - _Jennifer Voitle_, Nov 03 2026
%D A386683 J. H. Conway and R. K. Guy, Solution of a problem of Erdős, Colloq. Math. 20 (1969), 307.
%H A386683 P. Borwein and M. J. Mossinghoff, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-02-01460-6">Newman Polynomials with Prescribed Vanishing and Integer Sets with Distinct Subset Sums</a>, Math. Comp., 72 (2003), 787-800.
%H A386683 Paul W. Dyson, <a href="https://github.com/pwdyson/erdos_1">Distinct Subset Sum Search: Computation of 10th term of OEIS A276661</a>, GitHub, 2025.
%H A386683 W. F. Lunnon, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1988-0917837-5">Integer sets with distinct subset-sums</a>, Math. Comp. 50 (1988), 297-320.
%H A386683 Jennifer Voitle, <a href="/A386683/a386683.txt">Independent exhaustive search for n = 10: method, checks and C program</a>
